{
  "dom_id": "step-q53035",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14223346/assets/702edb8e1ea4a79ea12e891ed3874c36ff3e6628c9cb000753f74ca7bc35ec74.png)\n\nWhich of the figures above is a parallelogram?",
  "worked_solution": "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14223346/assets/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nRemember that a parallelogram is a quadrilateral with $2$ pairs of parallel sides.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each shape in turn.\n\n- The shape $P$ is a rectangle. Therefore, its opposite sides are parallel, so it is a parallelogram.\n\n- The shape $Q$ is a kite. It has a pair of opposite sides that are not parallel, therefore, it is not a parallelogram.\n\n- The shape $R$ is a rhombus. Since the opposite sides of a rhombus are parallel, it is a parallelogram.\n\n- The shape $S$ is a trapezoid. It has only one pair of parallel sides, therefore, it is not a parallelogram.\n\nTherefore, shapes $P$ and $R$ are parallelograms. The shapes $Q$ and $S$ are not parallelograms.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
